// =============================================================================
// std/builtin/type_aliases.code — Types that name something about types
// =============================================================================
//
// These are built-ins in the sense that the prelude re-exports them, so no
// module has to import them.
// =============================================================================

// A type that can never have an instance constructed, used as the result of
// a function that never returns.
//
// `never` is the compiler's own bottom type, and naming it here is what
// gives it the spelling the rest of the library uses. The alias is not
// cosmetic: the compiler knows a `never`-returning call diverges, so code
// after one is not required to produce a value.
//
// This was briefly written as `public enum Never {}` -- an uninhabited enum
// is the right shape, and it is what a language without a bottom type would
// have to do. It is wrong here for two reasons: enums are parsed but not
// yet lowered into the HIR at all, so the name did not resolve; and even
// once they are, an empty enum would be a type the compiler has no reason
// to believe is uninhabited, so `-> Never` would stop meaning "diverges".
public type Never = never;
